	"TECH_DESC_HIDDEN_SINGLE": "A digit that has only one place left in a row, column or box. The cell may hold other notes, but this digit can go nowhere else there, so it belongs here.",
	"TECH_DESC_LOCKED_CANDIDATES": "When all the places left for a digit in a box lie on one line, the digit must go on that line, so you can remove it from the rest of the line. The same works the other way round, from a line into a box.",
	"TECH_DESC_NAKED_PAIR": "Two cells in a row, column or box that hold the same two notes and nothing else. Those two digits belong to those two cells, so you can remove them from every other cell there.",
	"TECH_DESC_HIDDEN_PAIR": "Two digits that fit in only the same two cells of a row, column or box. Those cells must hold exactly those digits, so every other note in them can go.",
	"TECH_DESC_NAKED_TRIPLE": "Three cells in a row, column or box whose notes are drawn only from the same three digits. Those digits are used up by the three cells, so you can remove them from every other cell there.",
	"TECH_DESC_HIDDEN_TRIPLE": "Three digits that fit in only the same three cells of a row, column or box. Those cells must hold exactly those digits, so every other note in them can go.",
	"TECH_DESC_X_WING": "A digit that has just two possible places in each of two rows, both pairs in the same two columns. The digit must fill one diagonal of that rectangle, so it can be removed from the rest of those two columns (and the same with rows and columns swapped).",
	"TECH_DESC_XY_WING": "A hinge cell with two notes, X and Y, sees one cell with X and Z and another with Y and Z. Whatever the hinge turns out to be, one of those two cells ends up as Z, so a cell that sees both of them cannot be Z.",
	"TECH_DESC_XYZ_WING": "Like an XY-Wing, but the hinge cell holds all three notes, X, Y and Z, and sees two cells with XZ and YZ. Z ends up in one of the three cells whatever happens, so a cell that sees all three can drop the Z.",
	"TECH_DESC_SWORDFISH": "An X-Wing with three rows and three columns. If a digit's places in three rows all lie within the same three columns, it fills one cell in each row, so it can be removed from the rest of those columns.",
	"TECH_DESC_SIMPLE_COLORING": "Link a digit's pairs (rows, columns or boxes where it has only two places) into a chain, giving the cells two alternating colours. Exactly one colour is true, so a cell that sees both colours loses the digit, and a colour that meets itself is the false one.",
	"TECH_DESC_XY_CHAIN": "A chain of two-note cells in which each cell shares a note with the next. If the first end is not Z, the chain forces the last end to be Z, so one of the two ends is Z and any cell that sees both can drop it."
}
